WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.262
(violin music)

00:00:02.262 --> 00:00:05.496
- We're an offsite facility
of the Natural History Museum

00:00:05.496 --> 00:00:07.090
that's not open to the public.

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:09.691
This is the skull of a humpback whale.

00:00:09.691 --> 00:00:11.939
Our collection of marine mammals

00:00:11.939 --> 00:00:14.826
is the second largest
collection in North America.

00:00:14.826 --> 00:00:15.749
This hole right here

00:00:15.749 --> 00:00:18.473
is where the eye would have
been when it was still alive.

00:00:18.473 --> 00:00:21.605
We have approximately 5,000 specimens,

00:00:21.605 --> 00:00:26.438
and that includes dolphins,
whales, seals, sea lions.

00:00:26.438 --> 00:00:30.420
I consider myself the current
steward of the collection.

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:34.591
I think of the term whale
warehouse as kind of endearing.

00:00:34.591 --> 00:00:37.274
(chamber-style string music)

00:00:37.274 --> 00:00:39.265
When people come to the warehouse,

00:00:39.265 --> 00:00:41.966
definitely I think the first thing

00:00:41.966 --> 00:00:44.383
that is noticed is the smell.

00:00:45.529 --> 00:00:48.598
It has a musty museum smell,

00:00:48.598 --> 00:00:51.136
but, because of the marine mammals

00:00:51.136 --> 00:00:54.032
having a lot of oils and lipids,

00:00:54.032 --> 00:00:58.166
there's definitely a smell of rancid oils.

00:00:58.166 --> 00:01:01.652
We have jars filled with
various kinds of specimens.

00:01:01.652 --> 00:01:03.244
Ah yeah, this is nice.

00:01:03.244 --> 00:01:04.827
These are eyeballs.

00:01:06.089 --> 00:01:07.945
Most people I've warned in advance

00:01:07.945 --> 00:01:11.746
that, you know, this is what you need to
understand the place is like.

00:01:11.746 --> 00:01:14.246
You know, it's not a tourist attraction.

00:01:17.027 --> 00:01:19.692
The warehouse has several
different functions.

00:01:19.692 --> 00:01:23.109
And one is to serve as a storage facility

00:01:24.193 --> 00:01:26.489
for the oversized specimens.

00:01:26.489 --> 00:01:28.310
But it's also the facility

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:32.071
where we actually prepare new specimens.

00:01:32.071 --> 00:01:35.698
We're part of the National
Marine Mammal Stranding Network.

00:01:35.698 --> 00:01:38.574
We respond to dead
stranded marine mammals.

00:01:38.574 --> 00:01:41.447
And most of what we've
learned about these species

00:01:41.447 --> 00:01:44.530
is a result of studying them closely.

00:01:45.537 --> 00:01:47.783
We have specimens that have been around

00:01:47.783 --> 00:01:50.331
for decades or even more.

00:01:50.331 --> 00:01:53.608
The collection really is of
international significance.

00:01:53.608 --> 00:01:55.150
If you're a marine mammal researcher,

00:01:55.150 --> 00:01:57.021
you've probably been here.

00:01:57.021 --> 00:01:59.472
Some of the recent
research includes things

00:01:59.472 --> 00:02:01.889
like global climate processes

00:02:01.889 --> 00:02:04.806
and how that has changed over time.

00:02:06.217 --> 00:02:08.945
I'm a scientist, and
I'm a little bit removed

00:02:08.945 --> 00:02:11.174
from the emotional aspect of things,

00:02:11.174 --> 00:02:13.889
but I still appreciate the majesty

00:02:13.889 --> 00:02:17.795
of these incredibly large,
charismatic animals.

00:02:17.795 --> 00:02:21.878
And it's difficult to not
be affected emotionally

00:02:23.365 --> 00:02:25.859
by the kinds of things
that we're studying,

00:02:25.859 --> 00:02:29.242
animals that are on the
verge of extinction.

00:02:29.242 --> 00:02:31.909
What are we gonna do about that?

00:02:36.503 --> 00:02:40.336
(string music fades and ends)

